WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.310
From the lawsuit that took him out of comics,
to his journey to being one of the most highly

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:10.340
anticipated movie heroes of this decade, here's
the untold truth of Captain Ma -- er, Shazam!

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.820
After his debut in 1938, Superman was a huge
success.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.640
So successful, in fact, that he had inspired
a massive boom for the brand-new superhero

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.500
genre, including a huge wave of imitators
and ripoffs.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.480
Dozens of new titles hoped to create the next
Superman, but Fawcett Publications was one

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.600
publisher that actually managed to do it.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.930
In 1940, writer Bill Parker and artist C.C.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.340
Beck introduced the world to Captain Marvel,
a character who had all of Superman's powers,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.660
but with two key differences.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.290
First, Captain Marvel's origins were drawn
from a more magical, mythological source than

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.920
the sci-fi explanations behind Superman's
power.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.680
Second, and far more importantly, his mild-mannered
secret identity was a kid.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.760
Billy Batson, a homeless child was chosen
by the wizard Shazam.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.640
With a single magic word, he could transform
into an adult who wielded the powers of six

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.440
mythological heroes, turning him into the
World's Mightiest Mortal.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:04.640
"Say my name."

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.320
"Shazam!"

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.340
In other words, every kid who wished they
were Superman could now read the adventures

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.940
of a kid who could become Superman whenever
he wanted.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.080
With that idea, Captain Marvel was an instant
success.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.380
If you've got a good memory for trivia, you
might already know that Billy Batson and his

00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:30.509
magical alter-ego made their debuts in the
very first issue of Whiz Comics, but did you

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:33.140
know that was actually Whiz Comics #2?

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.700
That might seem confusing, but that's only
because it is.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.409
Before debuting a new title, publishers would
often create "ashcan" editions, cheap, low-quality

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:46.259
versions with low print runs that they could
use as proof-of-concept to attract potential

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.160
advertisers and secure a copyright.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.060
They'd done the same with Parker and Beck's
new character, then called Captain Thunder,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:57.040
but still hadn't settled on a title, publishing
it as both Thrill Comics and Flash Comics.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.980
By the time they got to publication, they'd
decided to go with Whiz.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.620
Since they'd already published the story in
the ashcan, Whiz started at #2… even though

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.660
they'd technically printed it twice already.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.620
Superman might've been the original, but as
the '40s went on, Captain Marvel quickly became

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.480
the most popular superhero of the decade by
outselling Superman and Action Comics.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.960
According to the Museum of Comic Book Advertising,
Captain Marvel Adventures would eventually

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.180
hit a high point of 1.3 million sales per
month.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.980
That's an impressive figure under any circumstances,
but consider that over the course of those

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.110
same years, Captain Marvel and his sidekicks
were also appearing in Whiz Comics, Wow Comics,

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.260
Master Comics, Captain Marvel Jr., Mary Marvel,
and The Marvel Family.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.080
In fact, he was so popular that Hoppy the
Marvel Bunny, a rabbit version of Captain

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.760
Marvel created for Fawcett's line of funny-animal
comics, was headlining two titles every month.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:54.780
For comparison, Superman and Batman each had
three, and one of them was World's Finest

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.640
Comics, the series they appeared in together.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.880
If you ever go back and read any Captain Marvel
comics from the '40s, it's not difficult to

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.920
figure out the secret of their success: they're
actually good.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.140
While a lot of Golden Age creators struggled
with figuring out the rules of the new medium

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.560
and the superhero genre, the Captain Marvel
stories presented fun, straightforward, and

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.000
well-structured adventures.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:16.640
C.C.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.700
Beck's art had always been fantastic, but
the arrival of writer Otto Binder was the

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.730
final ingredient needed to make Captain Marvel
great.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.580
Binder and Beck would become the team most
identified with the character, and in 1943,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.140
they created what is generally considered
to be one of the most important stories of

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.920
the Golden Age: "The Monster Society of Evil."

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.360
"Meet the Monster Society of Evil.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.280
The vilest villains of Fawcett City united.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.940
Together, we'll conquer the universe!"

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.140
It was an epic story that ran through a full
25 issues of Captain Marvel Adventures, the

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.040
first long-form, multi-part superhero story
in comics.

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.280
It was also the first story to feature a villainous
super-team made up of pre-existing villains

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.680
who united to take down the heroes, paving
the way for the modern-day event comic.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.100
As you might've already guessed, National
Comics, the company that would later become

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.130
DC, was not happy about Captain Marvel outselling
Superman.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.060
So unhappy, in fact, that they filed a lawsuit
against Fawcett that made it to court in 1948,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.020
alleging that they'd violated the copyright
on Superman by ripping him off for their own

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:19.520
character.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.120
Unfortunately for Fawcett, that actually is
what happened.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:24.620
"I idolized you.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:25.580
I wanted to be you.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.040
Whenever I was out there facing the bad guys,
I'd think 'what would Superman do?'"

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.690
In a much later interview, Roscoe Fawcett
would recall telling his staff "Give me a

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:36.340
Superman."

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.480
Even beyond that, the similarities were impossible
to ignore.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.060
Superman didn't have the market cornered on
super-strength and invulnerability, but the

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:49.880
cover to Whiz #2 featured Shazam tossing a
car, much like Superman on the cover of Action

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:50.880
Comics #1.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.220
In 1954, the courts ruled against Fawcett,
and they lost the rights to continue publishing

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.460
Captain Marvel stories.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:02.100
After the infamous lawsuit, Captain Marvel's
adventures ended, and while he wouldn't be

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.160
seen on the comics page again for almost two
decades, a character that was selling over

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.320
a million copies a month doesn't just vanish
from pop culture.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.560
Plenty of readers had fond memories of the
character, and plenty of publishers were eager

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.500
to recreate that level of success.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:20.240
In the United Kingdom, a publisher called
L. Miller and Son drafted artist Mick Anglo

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.940
to create an unofficial continuation of the
series.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.800
The character was renamed Marvelman, and the
magic word was changed to "Kimota."

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:33.080
Since the British printings of Captain Marvel
had ended at #24, Marvelman picked up at #25.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:40.200
It ran from 1954 to 1963, but in 1982, the
strip was revived in the pages of Warrior.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.810
It was rebooted in a much darker direction
by Alan Moore and Gary Leach, and served to

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.800
pave the way for later Moore projects like
Watchmen.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:54.280
In 1972, DC publisher Carmine Infantino decided
that Captain Marvel had been absent long enough.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.620
With that, DC licensed the characters from
Fawcett, and began publishing all-new adventures

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.480
of "the original Captain Marvel" in the pages
of Shazam!

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.440
The first issue was drawn by C.C.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.300
Beck, and featured Otto Binder, who had left
DC in 1969 to focus on sci-fi novels, as a

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.460
character in the first issue.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.639
The series would run for 35 issues, bringing
the Marvel Family to a new generation of readers

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.100
and incorporating them into the DC Multiverse.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.780
Readers weren't just reintroduced to the good
guys, though.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:25.980
In Shazam #28, Black Adam returned, an evil
version of Captain Marvel who had made his

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.880
only appearance 32 years earlier.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.120
While he lost that first fight, he'd go on
to become one of DC's most prominent villains,

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.360
and an occasional hero.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:41.900
When DC revived Captain Marvel in 1972, it
was in a series called Shazam, and by 1974,

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:45.620
they'd been prohibited from referring to their
lead character as "Captain Marvel" on the

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:46.560
cover at all.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:47.620
The reason?

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.500
An upstart superhero publisher called Marvel
Comics had snatched up the copyright on that

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.180
name for a new character they'd introduced
in 1967.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.440
Their Captain Marvel was a spacefaring soldier
of the Kree race named Mar-Vell who had come

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.500
to Earth on a mission of conquest and decided
to become a cosmic hero instead.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.260
In an interesting twist, Mar-Vell was revamped
in 1969 with a new red costume with a big

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:16.139
yellow insignia on the chest and a young sidekick,
Rick Jones, with whom he switched bodies in

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:18.340
a flash of light whenever a hero was needed.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:22.320
Of course, there would be other versions of
Captain Marvel that Marvel Comics would make,

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.720
including one that might be a bit more familiar
to movie-going audiences.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.520
Needless to say, the fact that both DC and
Marvel were publishing superheroes named "Captain

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.720
Marvel" caused no end of confusion.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:38.060
Eventually, when the DC Universe was rebooted
with the "New 52" in 2011, Billy Batson's

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.580
alter ego was rechristened as Shazam.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:46.440
When they revived Captain Marvel in the 1970s,
DC licensed the character from Fawcett Publications.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.460
In 1991, however, DC made the Shazam Family
a permanent part of their universe, acquiring

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.080
the rights to the character outright.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.160
At the same time, veteran creator Jerry Ordway
was assigned to reboot the character yet again

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.040
in the pages of a fully-painted graphic novel
called The Power of Shazam!

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:07.440
In addition to re-telling Billy Batson's origin,
this story also tied him more closely to Black

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:12.740
Adam, who had his own civilian identity: Theo
Adam, a greedy archaeologist who murdered

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.420
Billy's parents while on a dig.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:20.040
Released in 1994, The Power of Shazam was
an unqualified success, and led to the launch

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.360
of a monthly title of the same name the next
year.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.780
Ordway would write and provide covers for
the series, and draw a fair bit of it, too.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:31.740
With nearly fifty issues published, it was,
and is, the longest-running Shazam series

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.060
since 1954.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:38.690
After the DC Universe was rebooted for "The
New 52," Shazam started appearing as a backup

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.540
story in the pages of Justice League.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:46.171
In addition to a new costume and a new origin
for Dr. Sivana, this version of Billy Batson

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:49.600
wasn't quite the wholesome, good-natured kid
he'd been in the past.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:55.100
Instead, Geoff Johns and Gary Frank reimagined
him as far more bitter and cynical than readers

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.240
had seen before.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.760
The idea was that his initial suspicion and
self-interest would give way to a genuine

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.220
heroism and an affection for his adopted family.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.080
There was one other big twist, though.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:11.500
In the pages of Flashpoint, the universe-ending
event that led to the New 52 reboot, readers

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:16.440
got a glimpse of a version of Shazam who had
split his powers among six kids.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.580
This in itself wasn't a completely new idea,
Mary Batson and Freddy Freeman had been turned

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:27.000
into Mary Marvel and Captain Marvel Jr. respectively
the same way, as had a few other characters

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.720
all the way back in the Golden Age.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.100
This time, though, Billy decided to split
his powers not just with Mary and Freddy,

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.520
but with his foster siblings Darla, Pedro,
and Eugene.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.540
The result is a whole team of heroes with
a much more diverse cast than previous versions

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.700
of the Shazam Family, who will all be appearing
alongside each other in the pages of the next

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:46.710
Shazam title.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.860
It also feels a little reminiscent of a very
different superheroic captain:

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:57.420
"With their five powers combined, they summon
Earth's greatest champion, Captain Planet.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.300
Go Planet!"

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:05.500
Way back in 1941, Captain Marvel made his
big screen debut in a Republic Pictures serial,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:10.170
beating Max Fleischer's animated Superman
cartoons to the theater by six months and

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.280
predating a live-action Superman serial by
seven years.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:17.900
Now, in 2019, Billy Batson returns to the
movies in Shazam.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:24.700
With Asher Angel as Billy Batson, Zachary
Levi as Shazam, and Mark Strong as Dr. Sivana,

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.460
the film sticks pretty close to the 2012 version
of the hero.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.000
Weirdly enough, while Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson
has been attached to the project longer than

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.880
nearly anyone else, he doesn't appear in Shazam.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:40.610
He's been cast as Black Adam in DC's cinematic
universe, but Warner Bros. has announced that

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.830
they'll be featuring the character in a solo
film before he crosses paths with his old

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:45.500
nemesis.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:48.520
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.640
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.240
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

